\section{Threshold-type vocabulary}{

The data-driven detectors expose a \code{threshold_type} selecting how the
flag boundary is found from the data:
\itemize{
\item \code{"gap"} -- broken-stick gap on the sorted log-scores.
\item \code{"entropy"} -- density-valley (entropy) cut; safe on clean,
unimodal data.
\item \code{"significance"} / \code{"percentile"} -- parametric / quantile cuts
(probability detector).
\item \code{"auto"} -- self-determining cut (detour, speed cap).
\item \code{"fixed"} / \code{"hard"} -- a user-supplied literal threshold / cap.
}
}

\section{Consensus vocabulary}{

\code{\link[=mt_flag_consensus]{mt_flag_consensus()}} (and \code{mt_clean_track(consensus = )}) turn the
detectors' flags into a decision. The default is
\code{"evidence_corroborated"}: each detector emits a signed log-likelihood
ratio, these are calibrated commensurably into a \code{combined_evidence}
score, and a fix is flagged when the evidence is positive AND either two
detectors corroborate or the high-specificity detour detector is
saturated. Other modes: \code{"weighted_evidence"} (the smooth evidence score
thresholded directly), the Boolean \code{"class_aware"} (the prior default),
\code{"strict"}, \code{"majority"}, \code{"any"}, \code{"competence_count"}, and \code{"custom"}.
}

\section{Conventions}{

Functions are prefixed \code{mt_} when they take \code{move2} objects (raster /
scalar utilities are not). Preconditions are signalled with stable classed
conditions (\verb{move2utils_<context>_<reason>}); empty or non-finite geometry
is rejected rather than silently dropped. See \code{vignette(package = "move2utils")} for worked examples.
